Wheel scrolling runs on the main thread, as scrollbar dragging always does,
unless WEBKIT_ENABLE_THREADED_SCROLLING=1.

Threaded scrolling is switched on together with forced compositing (the
"always" hardware acceleration policy, which luakit uses).  The scrolling
thread then moves the page ahead of the main thread, and whenever the main
thread does not finish its rendering update within half a frame, sticky and
fixed elements are composited where the main thread last saw them: headers
jump by the scroll distance and back on every wheel step (github.com,
chatgpt.com, redhat.com), while dragging the scrollbar of the same page --
always a main-thread scroll -- stays steady.  Scrolling with the main thread
keeps the page and its fixed parts in step; compositing stays forced.

With no scrolling thread there is no scrolling coordinator either, so a
composited frame must not get the coordinated scrollbars controller, which
leaves drawing the scrollbars to the scrolling tree: the scrollbar would
still be draggable but never drawn.  Such a frame keeps the generic
controller, and the Adwaita theme paints its scrollbars into their layers
instead of leaving them to the (absent) scrolling thread.

